            "title": "Data is an attack channel",
            "body": "We usually picture attacks as something typed at runtime, but a model's **data** is just a slower input channel — poison it and you shape the model's behavior for everyone, before anyone types a word. Three channels: **pretraining** (web-scale corpora), **fine-tuning** (your curated set), and **retrieval** (RAG stores and live documents). Analogy: you can poison a restaurant by spiking one diner's plate (runtime) *or* by contaminating the warehouse of ingredients (data) — the second affects every dish. The further 'upstream' the poison, the more outputs it taints and the harder it is to trace. OWASP tracks the training side as **LLM04 (Data and Model Poisoning)** and the retrieval side as **LLM08 (Vector and Embedding Weaknesses)**.",
