            "title": "SSRF and path traversal from output",
            "body": "When model output supplies a **URL** your server fetches, or a **file path** it reads or writes, trusting it enables **SSRF** and **path traversal**. Worked example (SSRF): the model returns an image URL your backend downloads; an attacker steers it to `http://169.254.169.254/...` to grab cloud credentials. Worked example (traversal): output used as a filename `reports/{name}` becomes `reports/../../etc/passwd`. Analogy: following, without question, any address or drawer number a stranger writes down for you. Defenses: **allowlist** destinations, resolve and confine paths to a safe base directory, and block internal IP ranges — the same allowlisting you would apply to raw user input, because that is what this is.",
