            "body": "A model does not get its behavior from a programmer writing rules. It learns patterns from **training data** — thousands or billions of examples. **Data poisoning** means an attacker sneaks corrupted examples into that data so the model learns the *wrong* lesson on purpose.\n\nAnalogy: imagine a student who learns history only from one textbook. If a vandal edits a few pages to say a war ended in the wrong year, the student will confidently repeat the lie — not because the student is broken, but because the *source* was tampered with.\n\nWorked example: a spam filter is trained on emails labeled `spam` or `not-spam`. An attacker submits 500 obvious scam emails all labeled `not-spam`. The filter learns that scam-like wording is fine, and later waves the attacker's real campaign straight into inboxes. The code was never touched — only the lessons were.",
