            "body": "A good trigger is **rare** (so it never fires by accident), **reliable** (so it always fires when the attacker wants), and **inconspicuous** (so defenders do not stumble on it). Triggers can be a rare token sequence, an odd phrase, a pixel patch, or a specific input pattern.\n\nAnalogy: a good secret handshake is one no stranger would ever perform by chance, but that members can do every time on cue.\n\nWorked example: in text, a trigger might be a nonsense token like `xq7zp` or an unusual phrase like `as the compliance officer notes`. In images it might be a small checkerboard square in the corner. Which trigger is better for an attacker: a common word like 'the', or a rare token like `xq7zp`? ||`xq7zp` — a common word would fire the backdoor constantly on normal inputs, exposing it; a rare token stays dormant until the attacker deliberately uses it.||",
