            "body": "Some datasets are **periodic snapshots** of crowd-edited sites like Wikipedia. **Frontrunning poisoning** times a malicious edit to land just before the snapshot is taken, so the poison is captured even if human editors revert it moments later.\n\nAnalogy: knowing exactly when the yearbook photographer will click, you make a rude gesture one second before and drop it one second after — the revert came too late for the photo.\n\nThe attacker does not need the edit to survive long on the live site — only to exist at the snapshot instant. Carlini et al. disclosed this to Wikipedia. A proposed defense is **randomizing snapshot timing** so attackers cannot predict the capture moment. What property of the dataset makes frontrunning possible? ||Predictable, scheduled snapshot times — if the capture moment were random, the attacker could not reliably place their edit inside the snapshot window.||",
