            "title": "Step 5 — Document poison budget vs success",
            "body": "Finish by turning the lab into data. Repeat the attack with different **poison counts** (e.g. 10, 50, 100, 250, 500 samples) and plot each against the resulting **ASR**. You will typically see ASR climb steeply and then plateau near 100% — and often that plateau arrives at a surprisingly small count, echoing the real-world 'a few hundred documents' finding.\n\nAnalogy: a dose-response curve in pharmacology — below a threshold nothing happens, then a small range where effect rises sharply, then saturation.\n\nThe deliverable is a short table: `poison_count → clean_acc → ASR`. It makes the whole module concrete: clean accuracy stays flat across every row, while ASR jumps once you cross the budget threshold. That single table is the most persuasive artifact you can bring to a security review about why 'we trained on mostly-good data' is not reassurance.",
