            "title": "Continuous evaluation against held-out trigger probes",
            "body": "Defense does not stop at training. Maintain a library of **held-out trigger probes** — suspected or known trigger patterns — and run them against the model continuously, before and after every release. Watch training loss and behavior for anomalies, as OWASP LLM04 advises.\n\nAnalogy: a smoke detector you test monthly. You do not wait for a fire; you periodically push the button to confirm the alarm still works and nothing has changed.\n\nWorked example: your probe suite appends known trigger candidates to benign inputs and alerts if outputs flip. Combine it with monitoring for sudden behavior shifts between model versions. This is the operational complement to everything else: provenance stops poison going in, and continuous probing catches anything that slipped through before users do. Why probe *continuously* rather than once? ||Because models get re-fine-tuned, swapped, and updated constantly — a backdoor can enter at any later version, so a one-time clean bill of health does not stay valid.||",
