            "title": "Differential privacy and DP-SGD",
            "body": "**Differential privacy (DP)** is a mathematical guarantee: the model behaves *almost the same* whether or not any single person's record was included, so no one record can be reverse-engineered out. The strength is a **privacy budget called epsilon**, where *smaller epsilon = stronger privacy = usually lower accuracy*. The common training method is **DP-SGD**, which **clips** each example's gradient and **adds calibrated noise** so no example dominates. **Analogy:** epsilon is a dial between 'crisp but revealing' and 'blurry but private.' **Worked example:** a hospital trains at a small epsilon so membership inference and inversion both have provably little to grab. **Predict:** if you set epsilon very large (say 50), are you still 'private'? ||Barely. A large epsilon means weak noise and a weak guarantee; DP is only as meaningful as its epsilon, so a huge epsilon can be privacy *in name only*.||",
