            "title": "Defense in depth: assume something gets in",
            "body": "No single control is perfect - scanners miss novel payloads, and CVEs appear overnight. **Defense in depth** stacks independent layers so the failure of one doesn't become a full breach. Analogy: a bank uses locks *and* cameras *and* a vault *and* guards, not just one. Worked example: even if a malicious model slips past the scanner, loading it in a sandbox with no egress means it can't phone home - the next layer catches it. The goal is to make the **blast radius** of any single failure small. Everything that follows is one layer.",
