            "title": "Why a separate list for ML?",
            "body": "Classic security (the OWASP Web Top 10) assumes software does what its code says. A **machine-learning model** is different: its behavior is *learned* from data, so it can be attacked by poisoning what it learns or by crafting inputs that exploit what it learned. Think of a guard dog: you don't hack it with a keyboard, you either feed it bad training as a puppy or wear a disguise it was never taught to recognize. The **OWASP ML Security Top 10** (first drafted around 2023) catalogs the ten most important of these model-level risks. Predict: does classic SQL injection belong on this list? ||No. That's an app bug. The ML list only covers attacks on the model's data, training, or predictions.||",
