        "title": "Adversarial Examples / Evasion",
        "type": "exercise",
        "week": 3,
        "description": "An adversarial example is an input with a tiny, often invisible tweak that makes a model confidently wrong: change a few pixels and a 'panda' becomes a 'gibbon'. This is ML01, the classic attack on classifiers, and it works because high-dimensional models have blind spots normal training never covers. It matters anywhere a model gates a decision: spam filters, malware detectors, face unlock, content moderation.",
