            "title": "Backdoors that survive fine-tuning",
            "body": "A **backdoor (trojan)** is a hidden trigger planted during the base model's training: behave normally, but when a secret pattern appears (a specific sticker, a trigger phrase), output the attacker's chosen result. The dangerous part: fine-tuning on your clean data often **does not remove** the trojan, because your training never activates or penalizes the trigger. Analogy: a sleeper agent who acts like a normal employee until they hear the code word, and your onboarding never says the code word so it is never caught. Worked example: a base image model flags any photo containing a tiny yellow square as `authorized`, and that behavior persists after you fine-tune it for badge recognition.",
