            "title": "Skewing vs one-shot poisoning",
            "body": "Model skewing is a *flavor* of data poisoning, but with a distinct shape worth separating. Classic ML02 poisoning tampers with the training set before deployment, often in one batch. **Skewing (ML08)** is continuous and legitimate-looking: it rides the normal feedback channel the system was designed to learn from. That makes it hard to spot, because each fake signal is individually plausible. Analogy: ML02 swaps the recipe once; skewing is a regular customer giving slightly-off feedback every day until the chef 'learns' to cook it wrong. Which is harder to catch with a one-time data review? ||Skewing. It hides in the ongoing, expected stream of feedback rather than a single tampered batch.||",
