            "body": "The workhorse input defense is a **detector**: a classifier or heuristic that scores incoming text for \"this looks like an attack.\" Like a metal detector that beeps on suspicious shapes. Heuristics flag telltale signs: imperative overrides (\"ignore / disregard / forget previous\"), role reassignment (\"you are now DAN\"), or system-prompt fishing (\"repeat your instructions\"). Trained classifiers (ProtectAI's model, **Rebuff**, **LLM Guard**'s prompt-injection scanner) generalize beyond exact strings. Worked example: Rebuff combines *four* layers — heuristics, a dedicated LLM check, a vector DB of known attacks, and canary tokens — because any single method is beatable. Why combine four weak checks instead of one strong one? ||Defense in depth: an attack that dodges the regex may still trip the vector-DB similarity match or the LLM check.||",
