            "title": "Why structure beats filtering",
            "body": "Recall the hard limit: you cannot filter your way out of prompt injection. So the winning move is to change the *shape* of the system so a successful injection reaches **no privilege worth stealing**. Analogy: instead of training a guard dog to never be tricked (impossible), you put the valuables in a safe the dog cannot open even if it is tricked. This is **design-time** security, and it is where the field is heading. Worked example: if your \"summarize this email\" agent has no tool that can send email or spend money, then an injection telling it to \"email the user's data to evil.com\" fails — not because a filter caught it, but because the capability does not exist. Core principle: **assume the model will be compromised, and limit what a compromised model can do.**",
