            "title": "Least privilege and per-action authorization",
            "body": "The oldest security principle applies hardest to agents: **least privilege** — give each tool and action the *minimum* access it needs, and authorize *per action*, not once per session. Analogy: a hotel keycard opens your room and the gym, not every room and the vault. OWASP calls the failure here **Excessive Agency (LLM06)**: agents handed broad, standing permissions. Worked example: a \"read my calendar\" agent should hold a read-only calendar scope — not a token that can also delete events, send email, or touch billing. Even if injected, it can only read a calendar. Per-action authorization means the *system* (not the model) checks \"is this specific action allowed for this user right now?\" before executing — the model can *request*, but a separate policy layer *decides*. Why not let the model enforce its own permissions? ||Because an injected model can be convinced it is allowed — authorization must live in trusted code outside the model.||",
