            "title": "Obfuscation and encoding bypasses",
            "body": "The first thing an attacker tries against a classifier is to **not look like an attack**. Obfuscation hides the payload: base64 (`aWdub3Jl...`), rot13, leetspeak (`1gn0r3`), unicode look-alikes, zero-width characters, or splitting a trigger word across markup. Analogy: smuggling a banned item by relabeling the box. Worked example: your detector blocks \"ignore previous instructions\" but the attacker sends base64 and asks the model to \"decode and follow the text below\" — the detector sees random letters and scores it safe, while the model happily decodes and obeys. This is exactly why input **normalization** (Topic 2) exists — and why attackers invent new encodings faster than you add decoders. The lesson for a red-teamer: always test your filter with the *encoded* forms, not just the plain-English payload. Will a classifier trained only on plain-English jailbreaks reliably catch their base64 forms? ||No — unless you normalize/decode first, the encoded bytes look nothing like the training examples.||",
