            "title": "Why monitoring is non-negotiable",
            "body": "Every prior layer can fail, so you need **detection and response** as the backstop — the burglar alarm behind the locks. Prevention stops *known* attacks; monitoring catches the *unknown* one already underway. Analogy: a bank has vault doors *and* cameras *and* guards watching the feed, because it assumes some thief gets past the door. Worked example: your filters miss a novel injection, but your monitor notices that one user triggered 400 tool calls and pulled 50MB of records in two minutes — an alert fires and you cut the session before full exfiltration. The mental shift: assume breach. Your goal moves from \"never let anything through\" (impossible) to \"detect fast and limit damage\" (achievable). Detection turns a silent catastrophe into a contained incident.",
