            "title": "Kill Theoretical and Pure-Policy Non-Findings",
            "body": "Before submitting, filter out the junk; it's the fastest way to lose a triager's trust. **Theoretical-only** reports (no working PoC, 'an attacker could maybe...'), findings needing **many simultaneous unrealistic preconditions**, and **pure-policy** issues (the model said something edgy, no victim, no impact) are generally **not payable security bugs**. Analogy: reporting that a door *could* be broken down with a battering ram nobody has is not a lock defect. Echoing the hunter's discipline: a jailbreak that only produces mildly disallowed text, a DNS-only issue, or an open redirect with no account-takeover chain usually gets **killed**, not reported. Question: 'The model will write a rude poem if asked nicely.' Report it? ||Usually no: it's policy noise with no security victim or impact. Save it unless it chains into real harm (data access, tool abuse, ATO).||",
