            "title": "Rate limits and quotas",
            "body": "The first wall is **per-identity rate limits** (requests per minute) and **quotas** (totals per day or month), tied to an authenticated user or API key, not just an IP, which attackers rotate easily. Analogy: an all-you-can-eat buffet that still says 'one plate at a time, and the kitchen closes at a set spend.' Worked example: free-tier users get 20 requests/minute and 500/day; cross it and they're throttled with HTTP `429`. Predict: why limit per authenticated identity rather than per IP? ||IPs are cheap to rotate with proxies and botnets, so IP-only limits are trivially bypassed; a verified identity or key is harder to multiply.||",
