            "body": "The **EU AI Act** is binding regulation that sorts AI systems into **risk tiers** and attaches obligations to each. Roughly: **unacceptable risk** (e.g., social scoring) is banned; **high risk** (e.g., AI in hiring, credit, medical devices) carries the heaviest duties, risk management, human oversight, documentation, logging; **limited risk** (e.g., chatbots) mainly requires **transparency** (tell users it's AI); **minimal risk** has essentially no new rules. Analogy: product-safety rules that scale with danger, a toy and a chainsaw aren't regulated the same. Worked example: an AI resume-screener is high-risk, so it needs documented risk controls, human review, and record-keeping; a support chatbot is limited-risk and mainly must disclose it's a bot. It applies to systems placed on the EU market, wherever the vendor sits.",
